<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">market</ansi>

A market trades in a settlement's tracked goods, at prices that move
with its own stock, not a fixed shop price.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Usage:</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">market</ansi>
  Lists what's traded here: what you'd pay to buy each good, what the
  market pays you to sell it, and a rough stock level (<ansi fg="yellow">none</ansi>, <ansi fg="yellow">scarce</ansi>,
  <ansi fg="yellow">short</ansi>, <ansi fg="yellow">steady</ansi>, <ansi fg="yellow">plentiful</ansi>, or <ansi fg="yellow">glutted</ansi>).

  <ansi fg="command">market buy [good]</ansi>
  Buys one unit of a good, naming it by any close match to its name. A
  company already carrying all it can is refused before any gold
  changes hands (<ansi fg="command">help cargo</ansi>).

  <ansi fg="command">market sell [good]</ansi>
  Sells one unit of a good you carry.

Trading only works in the settlement's market room, not everywhere in
its zone; asking elsewhere tells you where it is.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Prices</ansi>

The market's buy and sell prices both come from its current stock: the
less it has, the more it charges to buy and the more it pays to sell;
the more it has, the cheaper it sells and the less it pays. Selling
pays about 20% less than buying costs at the same stock (the spread),
so buying and immediately selling back always loses money. Buying
takes a unit from stock; selling adds one. Every round (4 seconds), a
market's stock drifts a little toward its target level, so prices creep
back over time even if no one trades.

A market with poor standing toward your company charges more to buy and
pays less to sell, and one that shuns your company won't trade with it
at all; a black market may still deal with an outlaw company at
ordinary prices when the regular market won't. See <ansi fg="command">help standing</ansi>.

A rogue in your company haggles: up to 2% better prices per level, still
never enough to profit by buying and selling back (<ansi fg="command">help haggle</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Where</ansi>

Markets are found in a settlement's market square — Dunmar's Market
Square and Trappers' Post on the Old Kings Road are the ones known so
far. Not every settlement has one.

See also: <ansi fg="command">help standing</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help rumors</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help buy</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help sell</ansi>,
<ansi fg="command">help cargo</ansi>.
